--TEST--
A class that writes `Core\Json\Codec`'s `fromJson` and carries no `#[Json\Derive]` is decoded through the member it wrote, at every door and at every level of the document — `rule:core-classes/derive-generates-what-is-missing`, `rule:core-classes/derive-attribute`, spec § 6
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/derive-generates-what-is-missing`: the derive fills in the
// half a class does not write, so writing the decoding half is the opt-in as
// squarely as the attribute is — `nvs_types::derive`'s `check_json_sites`
// admits a call naming a class that records no field list but declares this
// member, and `nvs_stdlib::json`'s `hand_written` dispatches to it.
//
// § 6 declares the member over `mixed`, so what it is handed is the value the
// document held and not an object the field walk agreed to first. Re-encoding
// that value is the one reading that shows what arrived without this class
// claiming any shape of its own: a decode that quietly walked a field list
// instead, or that handed the member anything but the value at that position,
// fails every line below rather than printing plausibly.
class Version {
    public string $text;

    public function constructor(string $text)
    {
        $this->text = $text;
    }

    public static function fromJson(mixed $value): static
    {
        return new static(Core\Json::encode($value));
    }
}

// The class the call site named, whose document is an object — the shape the
// derived walk also takes, so this line alone does not tell the two doors
// apart.
echo "object=", Core\Json::decodeAs<Version>("{\"n\":1,\"name\":\"ada\"}")->text, "\n";

// The shape that does tell them apart: a derived walk refuses a document that
// is not a JSON object, and this door has no such question to ask.
echo "scalar=", Core\Json::decodeAs<Version>("\"plain\"")->text,
     " ", Core\Json::decodeAs<Version>("12")->text, "\n";

// `array<T>` is that decode run once per element, so the member is handed each
// element rather than the array — counted as well as printed, since a door
// that handed over the whole array once would still print a plausible line.
var $versions = Core\Json::decodeAs<array<Version>>("[1,\"two\",{\"three\":3}]");
string $listed = "";
int $elements = 0;
foreach ($versions as Version $one) {
    $listed = $listed . $one->text . "|";
    $elements = $elements + 1;
}
echo "list=", $listed, " count=", $elements, "\n";

// A document is a tree, so the door is at every level of it: a deriving class
// whose field is typed as the written one nests into that member, and so does
// each element of a list field.
#[Core\Json\Derive]
class Manifest {
    public Version $head;
    public array<Version> $rest;

    public function constructor(Version $head, array<Version> $rest)
    {
        $this->head = $head;
        $this->rest = $rest;
    }
}

var $manifest = Core\Json::decodeAs<Manifest>("{\"head\":{\"a\":1},\"rest\":[2,3]}");
string $tail = "";
foreach ($manifest->rest as Version $part) {
    $tail = $tail . $part->text . "|";
}
echo "nested=", $manifest->head->text, " rest=", $tail, "\n";

// The loose-values door reaches the same member with the array a program
// built, `Core\Arr::shapeAs` asking the class the same participation question
// `decodeAs` asks and answering it with the same dispatch.
echo "values=", Core\Arr::shapeAs<Version>(["n" => 1])->text, "\n";

// One written half is one direction. The encoding half is not consulted yet —
// `nvs_stdlib::json`'s own gap 1 — and this class carries no attribute for a
// derived encoder either, so writing one is still refused.
bool $encodes = true;
try {
    Core\Json::encode(new Version("x"));
} catch (LogicError $noEncoder) {
    $encodes = false;
}
echo "encodes=", ($encodes ? "yes" : "no"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
object={"n":1,"name":"ada"}
scalar="plain" 12
list=1|"two"|{"three":3}| count=3
nested={"a":1} rest=2|3|
values={"n":1}
encodes=no
